rrays, `TYPE_INFO.pBaseType` points to element type; `TYPE_INFO.diSize` maybe element count? Maybe.\nThere is `__GET_TYPE_SIZE` operator? Hmm.\n\nLet's search internal model memory: I recall CODESYS documentation examples:\n```\nVAR\n    xInfo : __SYSTEM.TYPE_INFO;\nEND_VAR\nxInfo := __GET_TYPE_INFO(__SYSTEM.TYPE_CLASS.TYPE_INT);\n```\nNot sure.\n\nMaybe use `__VARINFO(buffer).pType^` with a locally defined `TYPE ST_TypeInfo` that mirrors leading fields? Unsafe and version-dependent.\n\nLet's consider not using pointers at all if `VARIANT` has `VARIANT_TO_*` and we can store generic element bytes in a local `ARRAY[0..MAX] OF BYTE`. But still need read/write buffer.\n\nCould use CODESYS `MEMCPY` directly from `buffer` if `buffer` acts as pointer? `MEMCPY(ADR(buffer), ...)` would copy descriptor, not array. No.\n\nMaybe `VARIANT` supports direct `REF=`? `pBuffer := ADR(buffer);` then dereference as array? No.\n\nCould use `__VARINFO(buffer).pData` as array base and `__VARINFO(buffer).diSize / __VARINFO(item).diSize` for capacity—item element size same type. This is promising! We don't need separate element type metadata: element size equals size of item scalar, which we can get from `__VARINFO(item).diSize` (or TypeSize). The requirement says item concrete type must match array element type. So:\n```\nelemSize := __VARINFO(item).diSize;\ncapacity := __VARINFO(buffer).diSize / elemSize;\n```\nIf `__VARINFO(item)` on a VARIANT returns underlying scalar's size, and `__VARINFO(buffer)` returns underlying array total size. Then done. We can use pData of each. This avoids base type. Nice. If `__VARINFO` on variant describes descriptor rather than contents, both are same descriptor size and capacity = 1, bad; simulation may reveal. But likely compiler's variant metadata supports this.\n\nWhat about in-place item preservation: requirement P6 says item unchanged when no pop. We only write on pop. Good.\nStatus set 0 each scan then errors.\nRising edge: `enqEdge := enqueue AND NOT prevEnqueue`; same deq.\nReset priority: if reset then clear count, head, tail, status 0, edge memories = inputs? \"Edge memories update during reset; a request held afterward is not another edge.\" So set prevEnqueue := enqueue; prevDequeue := dequeue regardless. If reset and enqueue true, prev true, next scan still true no edge. Implementation:\n```\nstatus := 0;\nIF reset THEN\n  head:=0; tail:=0; elementCount:=0; initialized? keep;\nELSIF enqEdge AND NOT deqEdge THEN ...\nELSIF deqEdge AND NOT enqEdge THEN ...\nEND_IF\nprevEnqueue := enqueue;\nprevDequeue := dequeue;\n```\nIf both edges (invalid domain) maybe do nothing? Or process enqueue? Requirements caller must not; assumptions invalid domain. We can ignore/priority to enqueue? Doesn't matter for valid tests. Maybe avoid both operations: if both then status 0 unchanged. Formal LTL doesn't specify. Could choose enqueue first? Better not perform both.\n\nInitialization: On first invocation, derive capacity. Need buffer association fixed. We can initialize when `NOT initialized`:\n```\ninfoBuffer := __VARINFO(buffer);\ninfoItem := __VARINFO(item);\npBuffer := infoBuffer.pData;\npItem := infoItem.pData;\nelemSize := infoItem.diSize;\ncapacity := infoBuffer.diSize / elemSize;\ninitialized := TRUE;\n```\nBut `reset` initial call? reset first scan should still derive. Fine. Also outputs initial before first invocation must be 0; FB default outputs 0.\n\nPotential issue item pData could change every scan (caller variable address likely fixed; could be a temp? IN_OUT is reference, fixed). Query each call safer. `pItem := __VARINFO(item).pData;`. Buffer fixed; query at init. We can query both each scan.\n\nElement copy: `MEMCPY(pBuffer + tail * elemSize, pItem, elemSize)`. CODESYS pointer arithmetic: `pBuffer + offset` may be pointer arithmetic? In ST, POINTER TO BYTE + integer might not be allowed; use `pBuffer[offset]` or `ADR(pBuffer[offset])`. But pBuffer is pointer. `pBuffer + offset` is likely not valid. COD