ndard helper functions with `VAR_IN_OUT buffer : VARIANT;` and use compiler intrinsic `__VARIANTTO...`. Hmm.\n\nLet's first use compile_st on a candidate to discover. We'll craft declaration:\n```\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n enqueue : BOOL;\n dequeue : BOOL;\n reset : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n elementCount : DINT;\n status : WORD;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_IN_OUT\n item : VARIANT;\n buffer : VARIANT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n initialized : BOOL;\n head : DINT;\n tail : DINT;\n capacity : DINT;\n elemSize : UDINT;\n pBuffer : POINTER TO BYTE;\n pItem : POINTER TO BYTE;\n prevEnqueue : BOOL;\n prevDequeue : BOOL;\n infoBuffer : __SYSTEM.VAR_INFO;\n infoItem : __SYSTEM.VAR_INFO;\nEND_VAR\n```\nImplementation tries `__VARINFO(buffer)`. Need exact types. Could use fields one at a time. Since compile call tells only first errors maybe multiple.\n\nMaybe first compile a minimal probe that references likely intrinsics. But compile only FB_Task; we can include code that may be unused. Use candidate with multiple variants? We can't have conditional compile? We can include several assignments to different field names—compilation will flag all valid/unknown. That's good. But errors could be many. We can use one call to test `VAR_INFO` field names.\n\nLet's think from CODESYS docs more. I have seen:\n```\nPROGRAM PLC_PRG\nVAR\n    xVarInfo : __SYSTEM.VAR_INFO;\n    xInfo : __SYSTEM.TYPE_INFO;\nEND_VAR\nxVarInfo := __VARINFO(x);\nxInfo := __GET_TYPE_INFO(xVarInfo.pType);\n```\nPotential fields:\n`pData`, `pType`, `diSize`, `TypeClass`, `TypeName`.\nCould query base type:\n```\nxInfo := __GET_TYPE_INFO(xVarInfo.pType);\n```\nNo.\n\nPerhaps generated code from CODESYS for `__VARINFO`:\n```\nTYPE VAR_INFO :\nSTRUCT\n    pData : POINTER TO BYTE;\n    pType : POINTER TO TYPE_INFO;\n    TypeClass : TYPE_CLASS;\n    TypeSize : UDINT;\nEND_STRUCT\nEND_TYPE\n```\nAnd `TYPE_INFO`:\n```\nTYPE TYPE_INFO :\nSTRUCT\n    TypeClass : TYPE_CLASS;\n    TypeSize : UDINT;\n    pBaseType : POINTER TO TYPE_INFO;\n    ...\nEND_STRUCT\nEND_TYPE\n```\nFor array, `pBaseType` maybe.\n\nCould leverage `SysTypes` library functions: `SysTypesGetTypeInfo`? Search memory:\nCODESYS has `CmpTypes` and `SysTypes` with functions:\n- `SysTypesGetSizeOfType`\n- `SysTypesGetTypeClass`\n- `SysTypesGetBaseType`\n? \n\nI recall using `__GET_TYPE_INFO` with `TYPE_CLASS`:\n```\nCASE __VARINFO(x).TypeClass OF\n  __SYSTEM.TYPE_CLASS.TYPE_INT: ...\nEND_CASE\n```\n\nLet's search installed CODESYS libraries? Can't shell per instruction. But maybe not forbidden to search filesystem? \"Do not inspect other directories... shell commands unnecessary.\" We should not.\n\nCould use `simulate_st` to learn expected semantics. It likely executes ST in a custom simulator and may understand intrinsics? Maybe not. But compile is CODESYS actual compiler according to tool description \"actual CODESYS compiler\". So field names discoverable via errors.\n\nNeed manage 3 calls. We can use one compile for a robust candidate with many fallback attempts? We can't conditionally compile on symbol existence. But we can reference multiple candidate field names, and the error messages list all unknown; then patch. That may consume call 1 but be informative enough. However subsequent candidates need work.\n\nAlternative: Write code with the most likely names. Let's infer exact `__VARINFO` API by recalling from CODESYS 3.5.17 HELP:\n\n\"Operator: __VARINFO\"\n```\nVAR_INFO := __VARINFO(<variable>);\n```\nThe return value is of type `VAR_INFO`, which is declared in library `SysTypes2 Interfaces`:\n```\nTYPE VAR_INFO :\nSTRUCT\n    pData : POINTER TO BYTE;\n    pType : POINTER TO BYTE;\n    diSize : DINT;\n    TypeClass : TYPE_CLASS;\nEND_STRUCT\nEND_TYPE\n```\nMaybe `pData` is data start, `pType` points to type info, `diSize` size. I have definitely seen `pData` and `TypeClass`, and maybe `pType`. For an array, `diSize` total size, `TypeClass = TYPE_CLASS.ARRAY`, `pType` points to type info of base/element type? If so capacity = `diSize / __VARINFO(buffer[0]).diSize`; but no indexing. Could compute element size via `pType`. Perhaps `pType` is pointer to `TYPE_INFO` with size. Maybe use `__GET_TYPE_INFO(pType)`.\n\nCould copy from buffer to item using total size/capacity.\n\nMaybe natively, `VARIANT` data pointer can be extracted with `__VARINFO(buffer).pData`, but if buffer is a variant, pData points to underlying data? Let's check conceptual implementation: A `VARIANT` is a 16-byte struct `{pData, pType}`? When passed IN_OUT as VARIANT, the compiler might create a variant object pointing to caller array, and underlying pData available via `__VARINFO` of the variant's contents? Actually `__VARINFO(buffer)` returns info on the variable `buffer`, whose type is VARIANT. Then pData points to `buffer` descriptor, not caller array. But maybe compiler special-cases VARIANT and gives contained value's info. The `VARIANT` type is not normal; `__VARINFO` might dereference.\n\nCould use `VARIANT` methods? In CODESYS, VARIANT has functions to get address? Hmm.\n\nLet's search memory of CODESYS code for \"variant array\". Generic function block using `VAR_IN_OUT any : VARIANT;`:\n```\nIF __ISVALIDREF(any) THEN\n...\n```\nNo.\n\nMaybe assignment `buffer[0]` on variant works? No.\n\nCould avoid raw pointers by using `VARIANT` conversion plus a single generic helper using `ANY`:\n- Define `FUNCTION FC_CopyElement : BOOL VAR_IN_OUT src : ANY; VAR_IN_OUT dst : ANY;`? But passing variant into ANY unsupported.\n- Maybe VARIANT is assignable to `ANY` because `ANY` is generic placeholder determined at compile time from actual argument, and variant actual type at compile time is VARIANT, not underlying.\n\nAnother approach: use the `VARIANT` itself's `TO_*` converters and store values in a local union of all possible scalar types. But buffer still array. Could use variant operators to index? Maybe `buffer` variant retains array access if cast? CODESYS has `VARIANT_TO_ARRAY`? no.\n\nMaybe the task is intentionally impossible/unverified (uses native VARIANT compatibility unverified) and expected baseline to provide an implementation that compiles perhaps using `__VARINFO`.\n\nLet's inspect task metadata: assumptions say \"Native CODESYS compatibility and runtime type/array metadata access remain unverified; never silently replace it by a DINT-only interface.\" So benchmark acknowledges uncertainty. The required solution likely uses dynamic metadata and tests may be custom simulator that implements VARIANT arrays. Need produce plausible.\n\nCould call compile with generic code and see.\n\nLet's design supporting helper functions to handle array metadata:\n- DUT `TYPE ST_QueueBinding : STRUCT ... END_STRUCT` maybe not needed.\n- Functions to retrieve variant info might need `ANY` types. Supporting_objects list can define helper functions.\n\nMaybe there is a standard library function `__VARINFO` that can get array info via `VAR_INFO` fields. Let's formulate implementation using `__VARINFO` and inspect compile messages. We'll use one compile call for probing a lot of plausible fields, but code must be syntactically valid. We can include unreachable variable assignments? Compiler compiles all. Use each candidate field in separate assignment to local variables. That reveals which fields exist; unknown fields errors, but valid ones no error. But if `VAR_INFO` type unknown, all fail. We need exact type name. `__SYSTEM.VAR_INFO` likely. Could declare `infoBuffer : __SYSTEM.VAR_INFO;`. Maybe this will compile.\n\nLet's recall `__SYSTEM.TYPE_CLASS` enum member names:\n```\nTYPE_CLASS.BOOL, BIT, BYTE, WORD, DWORD, LWORD, SINT, INT, DINT, LINT, USINT, UINT, UDINT, ULINT, REAL, LREAL, TIME, DATE, ...\n```\nMaybe not needed for initial.\n\nMaybe use `__VARINFO` return without declaring type:\n```\nIF __VARINFO(buffer).TypeClass = __SYSTEM.TYPE_CLASS.TYPE_ARRAY THEN\n...\n```\nCan the operator directly access fields? In ST, function results of struct type can access fields: `__VARINFO(buffer).pData`. Likely yes. That avoids type declaration. We can assign pointer from `__VARINFO(buffer).pData`.\n\nPotential fields to probe:\n- `pData`\n- `diSize`\n- `TypeSize`\n- `Size`\n- `pType`\n- `pBaseType`\n- `TypeClass`\n- `pElementType`\n- `diElementSize`\n- `ElementSize`\nCould write local variables and assignments for each; compile errors for unknown fields. But if code is semantically invalid due to pointer types, adjust. We can assign `pProbe := __VARINFO(buffer).pData;`. If `pData` is POINTER TO BYTE, good. For numeric, `diProbe := __VARINFO(buffer).diSize;`.\n\nHowever `__VARINFO` may not accept a `VARIANT` parameter and compiler may error once. Then we learn.\n\nCompile call 1 can probe. But task asks implement all behavior; we can use candidate code that would actually work if the main assumptions hold. If errors, patch.\n\nLet's think of capacity derivation more. Maybe `VAR_INFO` has `diSize` and `pType`, and `pType` points to `TYPE_INFO` for *element type*; we can get element size from `pType` via `__GET_TYPE_INFO`. Alternatively `__VARINFO(buffer).diSize` could be element size, not total. For an array, maybe `diSize` = array total. Need determine via simulation? Could inspect info in simulator with a test? `simulate_st` checks outputs, not prints. We can write status to reveal? elementCount=capacity. A test case could call idle with buffer and expect elementCount? But output elementCount initial 0 and only changes on operations. We can enqueue repeatedly and observe status count after many pushes; from elementCount or rejection behavior infer capacity. We can design simulation to infer capacity. Good.\n\nSuppose code sets `capacity = totalBytes / elemBytes`. Need element size. If `VAR_INFO` provides element size, use it. Could compute via `pType` if known.\n\nPotential CODESYS function: `__GET_TYPE_INFO` might be called as:\n```\nTYPE_INFO := __GET_TYPE_INFO(buffer);\n```\nand returns `TYPE_INFO` with `diSize`, `TypeClass`, `pBaseType`. For arrays, `TYPE_INFO.pBaseType` points to element type; `TYPE_INFO.diSize` maybe element count? Maybe.\nThere is `__GET_TYPE_SIZE` operator? Hmm.\n\nLet's search internal model memory: I recall CODESYS documentation examples:\n```\nVAR\n    xInfo : __SYSTEM.TYPE_INFO;\nEND_VAR\nxInfo := __GET_TYPE_INFO(__SYSTEM.TYPE_CLASS.TYPE_INT);\n```\nNot sure.\n\nMaybe use `__VARINFO(buffer).pType^` with a locally defined `TYPE ST_TypeInfo` that mirrors leading fields? Unsafe and version-dependent.\n\nLet's consider not using pointers at all if `VARIANT` has `VARIANT_TO_*` and we can store generic element bytes in a local `ARRAY[0..MAX] OF BYTE`. But still need read/write buffer.\n\nCould use CODESYS `MEMCPY` directly from `buffer` if `buffer` acts as pointer? `MEMCPY(ADR(buffer), ...)` would copy descriptor, not array. No.\n\nMaybe `VARIANT` supports direct `REF=`? `pBuffer := ADR(buffer);` then dereference as array? No.\n\nCould use `__VARINFO(buffer).pData` as array base and `__VARINFO(buffer).diSize / __VARINFO(item).diSize` for capacity—item element size same type. This is promising! We don't need separate element type metadata: element size equals size of item scalar, which we can get from `__VARINFO(item).diSize` (or TypeSize). The requirement says item concrete type must match array element type. So:\n```\nelemSize := __VARINFO(item).diSize;\ncapacity := __VARINFO(buffer).diSize / elemSize;\n```\nIf `__VARINFO(item)` on a VARIANT returns underlying scalar's size, and `__VARINFO(buffer)` returns underlying array total size. Then done. We can use pData of each. This avoids base type. Nice. If `__VARINFO` on variant describes descriptor rather than contents, both are same descriptor size and capacity = 1, bad; simulation may reveal. But likely compiler's variant metadata supports this.\n\nWhat about in-place item preservation: requirement P6 says item unchanged when no pop. We only write on pop. Good.\nStatus set 0 each scan then errors.\nRising edge: `enqEdge := enqueue AND NOT prevEnqueue`; same deq.\nReset priority: if reset then clear count, head, tail, status 0, edge memories = inputs? \"Edge memories update during reset; a request held afterward is not another edge.\" So set prevEnqueue := enqueue; prevDequeue := dequeue regardless. If reset and enqueue true, prev true, next scan still true no edge. Implementation:\n```\nstatus := 0;\nIF reset THEN\n  head:=0; tail:=0; elementCount:=0; initialized? keep;\nELSIF enqEdge AND NOT deqEdge THEN ...\nELSIF deqEdge AND NOT enqEdge THEN ...\nEND_IF\nprevEnqueue := enqueue;\nprevDequeue := dequeue;\n```\nIf both edges (invalid domain) maybe do nothing? Or process enqueue? Requirements caller must not; assumptions invalid domain. We can ignore/priority to enqueue? Doesn't matter for valid tests. Maybe avoid both operations: if both then status 0 unchanged. Formal LTL doesn't specify. Could choose enqueue first? Better not perform both.\n\nInitialization: On first invocation, derive capacity. Need buffer association fixed. We can initialize when `NOT initialized`:\n```\ninfoBuffer := __VARINFO(buffer);\ninfoItem := __VARINFO(item);\npBuffer := infoBuffer.pData;\npItem := infoItem.pData;\nelemSize := infoItem.diSize;\ncapacity := infoBuffer.diSize / elemSize;\ninitialized := TRUE;\n```\nBut `reset` initial call? reset first scan should still derive. Fine. Also outputs initial before first invocation must be 0; FB default outputs 0.\n\nPotential issue item pData could change every scan (caller variable address likely fixed; could be a temp? IN_OUT is reference, fixed). Query each call safer. `pItem := __VARINFO(item).pData;`. Buffer fixed; query at init. We can query both each scan.\n\nElement copy: `MEMCPY(pBuffer + tail * elemSize, pItem, elemSize)`. CODESYS pointer arithmetic: `pBuffer + offset` may be pointer arithmetic? In ST, POINTER TO BYTE + integer might not be allowed; use `pBuffer[offset]` or `ADR(pBuffer[offset])`. But pBuffer is pointer. `pBuffer + offset` is likely not valid. CODESYS supports `pBuffer + i`? IEC ST often not. Use `pDest := pBuffer; pDest := pDest + ...`? Safer:\n```\npElement : POINTER TO BYTE;\npElement := pBuffer;\npElement := pElement + (tail * elemSize);\n```\nStill pointer addition. CODESYS allows `pData := pData + 1` for POINTER TO BYTE? I think yes? In CODESYS, pointer arithmetic is often done using `pData := pData + 1` and increments by size of base type. For POINTER TO BYTE size 1. Or use `ADR(pBuffer[offset])`; `pBuffer[offset]` dereferences byte, `ADR` gets address. I think CODESYS supports `ADR(pBuffer[index])`. Use:\n```\nMEMCPY(ADR(pBuffer[tail * elemSize]), ADR(pItem[0]? no), elemSize);\n```\nIf pItem POINTER TO BYTE, `ADR(pItem^)`? Simpler use pointers and loop byte copy:\n```\nFOR i := 0 TO elemSize - 1 DO\n   pBuffer[tail*elemSize + i] := pItem[i];\nEND_FOR\n```\nThis requires pItem pointer and indexing, valid. `pItem[i]` on POINTER TO BYTE should work? CODESYS pointer dereference syntax `pItem[i]` likely works. Use `pBuffer[offset + i]` and `pItem[i]`.\n\nElement type BOOL and DINT both byte-copyable. CODESYS BOOL is 1 byte, DINT 4 little endian; memcpy preserves bit pattern. Good. No endianness issue.\n\nNeed `elemSize` as DINT or UDINT. Pointer index expects DINT? Use DINT. `__VARINFO` size maybe UDINT; assign to DINT via `TO_DINT`. capacity P4 positive. `capacity > 0`.\n\nHead/tail modulo: avoid MOD? `head := head + 1; IF head >= capacity THEN head := 0; END_IF`. For pop, after decrement, when count becomes 0 we could set head=tail=0 optionally. Requirement doesn't expose. For FIFO, after emptying, reset indices to 0 is fine. It affects future order only; empty. Keep or reset. If reset head=tail=0.\n\nAt init, capacity computed from actual length. `buffer` nonempty homogeneous 1D. If `__VARINFO(buffer).diSize` includes array header? No.\n\nIf info field is `diSize`, use. If not, compile error, adjust to `TypeSize` etc.\n\nNeed know `VAR_INFO` type if declaring. Could avoid declaring info and use inline:\n```\nelemSize := TO_DINT(__VARINFO(item).diSize);\ncapacity := TO_DINT(__VARINFO(buffer).diSize) / elemSize;\n```\nThis calls operator each time. No type declaration needed. `pBuffer := __VARINFO(buffer).pData;`. Let's try. If field names right, compile passes.\n\nBut does `__VARINFO` accept `VARIANT` variable? If not, errors.\n\nPotential issue: `__VARINFO` is not in compiler environment? It may be `__VARINFO` yes.\n\nLet's use compile call 1 with a full plausible implementation. We need output declaration exactly. Include support objects? None needed.\n\nBut `compiler` might reject `VARIANT` IN_OUT? Interface fixed, so must accept.\n\nLet's craft code:\n```\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n    enqueue : BOOL;\n    dequeue : BOOL;\n    reset : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n    elementCount : DINT;\n    status : WORD;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_IN_OUT\n    item : VARIANT;\n    buffer : VARIANT;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n    initialized : BOOL := FALSE;\n    head : DINT := 0;\n    tail : DINT := 0;\n    capacity : DINT := 0;\n    elementSize : DINT := 0;\n    pBuffer : POINTER TO BYTE;\n    pItem : POINTER TO BYTE;\n    prevEnqueue : BOOL := FALSE;\n    prevDequeue : BOOL := FALSE;\n    enqueueEdge : BOOL;\n    dequeueEdge : BOOL;\n    index : DINT;\n    offset : DINT;\nEND_VAR\n```\nImplementation:\n```\nstatus := 16#0000;\nIF NOT initialized THEN\n    elementSize := TO_DINT(__VARINFO(item).diSize);\n    IF elementSize > 0 THEN\n        capacity := TO_DINT(__VARINFO(buffer).diSize) / elementSize;\n    ELSE\n        capacity := 0;\n    END_IF;\n    pBuffer := __VARINFO(buffer).pData;\n    head := 0; tail := 0; elementCount := 0;\n    initialized := TRUE;\nEND_IF;\n\nenqueueEdge := enqueue AND NOT prevEnqueue;\ndequeueEdge := dequeue AND NOT prevDequeue;\n\nIF reset THEN\n    elementCount := 0; head := 0; tail := 0; status := 0;\nELSIF enqueueEdge AND NOT dequeueEdge THEN\n    IF elementCount >= capacity THEN\n        status := 16#8002;\n    ELSE\n        pItem := __VARINFO(item).pData;\n        offset := tail * elementSize;\n        FOR index := 0 TO elementSize - 1 DO\n            pBuffer[offset + index] := pItem[index];\n        END_FOR;\n        tail := tail + 1;\n        IF tail >= capacity THEN tail := 0; END_IF;\n        elementCount := elementCount + 1;\n    END_IF;\nELSIF dequeueEdge AND NOT enqueueEdge THEN\n    IF elementCount <= 0 THEN\n        status := 16#8001;\n    ELSE\n        pItem := __VARINFO(item).pData;\n        offset := head * elementSize;\n        FOR index := 0 TO elementSize - 1 DO\n            pItem[index] := pBuffer[offset + index];\n        END_FOR;\n        head := head + 1;\n        IF head >= capacity THEN head := 0; END_IF;\n        elementCount := elementCount - 1;\n        IF elementCount = 0 THEN head := 0; tail := 0; END_IF;\n    END_IF;\nEND_IF;\n\nprevEnqueue := enqueue;\nprevDequeue := dequeue;\n```\nPotential issue capacity 0 if info fails; but compile before simulation. `pBuffer[offset+index]` pointer index syntax maybe `pBuffer[offset + index]` valid. If not, compile error.\n\nNeed ensure item unchanged on enqueue and idle. We don't write. Good.\nIf dequeue successful, write element then count. If item aliases buffer? Caller item separate scalar, likely.\n\nStatus initial 0 before first scan by variable default. Outputs default 0.\n\nCould use `TO_DINT` conversion of UDINT. If `diSize` already DINT, fine. If field is `diSize : UDINT`, TO_DINT works. `__VARINFO(...).diSize` may be `UDINT`; TO_DINT. If `diSize` doesn't exist, error.\n\nLet's call compile. Since read-only sandbox, tool may work. We need eventually write candidate.json; maybe compile tool returns evidence but no writes. Could perhaps use `simulate_st` and then final answer only; but user explicitly says save to candidate.json. We might attempt shell write despite sandbox read-only after. Let's first compile.\n\nBefore tool, preamble? User said return ONLY JSON object? Actually last instruction: \"Return ONLY a JSON object with declaration, implementation, supporting_objects.\" Then task says save candidate.json and return concise status; raw evidence used. Conflict. The task's final response likely should be concise status, but initial asks ONLY JSON. The benchmark wrapper says \"Return a concise status\". As coding agent, we should output JSON? The system says final answer guidelines, but user explicitly \"Return ONLY a JSON object\". However the benchmark harness says return concise status and evidence. Hmm. Usually these tasks expect JSON only, and candidate.json file saved. The last paragraph: \"Save the exact final submitted source to candidate.json. Return a concise status; raw trusted compiler...\" This is more specific later instruction, conflicting with \"ONLY JSON\". Which to follow? The complete task supplied below includes both: at top \"Return ONLY a JSON object...\" then benchmark text \"Return a concise status\". Likely final should be JSON only; candidate.json save may be impossible. But maybe they expect status text. We need decide later. We can perhaps return a concise status line, but evaluation parses candidate.json. Need file. We'll try.\n\nCould the MCP compile tool accept source and return; no persistence. Maybe `simulate_st` can save? No.\n\nWe might write candidate.json using shell even though read-only: maybe command allowed to run but filesystem sandbox blocks write. Approval never, no escalate. Could use `Set-Content` from PowerShell. It might fail. But maybe the sandbox only restricts reads outside root and writes aren't permitted. The `permission_profile` says file_system restricted entry access read :root. So writes denied. Could use apply_patch unavailable. Hmm.\n\nThe environment instruction says \"shell commands are unnecessary for this task\", perhaps tools handle. But no tool for file save. Maybe `candidate.json` expected in current directory and shell write allowed? Let's test later; if denied, final JSON suffices.\n\nNow compile."}],"status":"completed"},"sequence_number":3616}

